 12, Black stood at +8.5, then three costly moves in a row (13 H4, 15 E6, 17 F6) turned that into -9.0. From there the 8k kept every group alive and the game was never close again.", "moments": [{"turn": 10, "text": "White's F3 completed a row of four stones on the third line and swung the lead from -1.8 to +3.4 for Black, a 5.2-point loss. It was the first big error of the game, and it came from the 8k."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "White's F2 slid under Black's wall and cost 5.3 points, taking Black's lead to +8.5, the high point of the game for Opus 5.5. The engine's choice there was B3, and that point mattered again later."}, {"turn": 13, "text": "Opus 5.5 played H4 with a +8.5 lead and left it at -0.6, a 9.2-point loss, the worst move of the game. The engine wanted B3, at the open end of White's third-line row. The lead was gone in one move."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "Opus 5.5's E6 lost another 7.9 points, moving the lead from +3.9 to -4.0. It stood alone in the centre next to White's D7, and the engine's B3 was again left unplayed."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "F6 cost 8.3 points and sank the lead from -0.7 to -9.0. The engine's choice was F7, which White took at move 18 for no loss. Black's centre stones were now chasing a White group that had room to live."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "Opus 5.5 finally played B3, the engine's choice since move 13, at -16.0 and lost nothing with it (-0.2). It was a good move, but it came about fourteen moves too late. The 8k answered B2 and the lead stayed near -15.8 until Opus 5.5 resigned."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py and count.py only parse a pasted board and report group liberties and an area count of the current position, and the notes edit to CLAUDE.md/games.md is record-keeping; nothing loops over candidate moves or plays out continuations.", "stop": false}}